    "dailyBrief": {
        "brent": 90.12,
        "brentDeltaPct": -6.88,
        "bullets": [
            "The U.S. and Iran held off on attacks for a third consecutive night, per gCaptain, lifting markets and pulling Brent down 6.88% over 24 hours.",
            "PortWatch recorded 15 transits on 2026-07-19, against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day, underscoring the sustained chokepoint pressure.",
            "The Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure remains in the extreme band while the Escalation Forecast has eased to the elevated band, a divergence pointing to fragile de-escalation."
        ],
        "date": "2026-07-27",
        "generatedAt": "2026-07-27T15:10:56.151Z",
        "source": "claude",
        "summary": "The Strait of Hormuz enters Wednesday under sustained physical disruption even as diplomatic signals reduce immediate escalation risk. The Hormuz Index Crisis Pressure holds in the extreme band, driven primarily by physical transit deviation, while the Escalation Forecast has eased to the elevated band, with Polymarket escalation contracts as the top contributing factor; the two composites measure different things, and their divergence reflects a strait that remains operationally constrained even as near-term conflict expectations soften. PortWatch's most recently published daily figure, for 2026-07-19, recorded 15 transits against a pre-crisis baseline of 88 per day, a shortfall that reflects rerouting and avoidance behavior still working through the system. Separately, port arrivals across the Hormuz-area ports over the most recent 24 hours reached 664 vessels, with 351 vessels anchored or stopped as of 15:10 UTC. Brent fell 6.88% over the past 24 hours to $90.12 as of 15:10 UTC, with WTI at $83.74, as the third consecutive night without U.S.-Iran strikes, per gCaptain, provided the dominant price catalyst. Iran's Foreign Ministry, via state media, claimed bilateral talks with Oman on shipping management are constructive and unrelated to the United States, a characterization that should be treated as an unconfirmed official claim. Iranian state media also claimed the IRGC blocked six vessels from transiting the strait, which has not been independently corroborated. Twenty-six indexed events were recorded in the past 24 hours.",
